course: history
lesson: 62
title: Everyday inventions: pots, boats, wheels, horses, clocks and glasses
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Owen's game hands him «The Wheel» as one of the first techs, so he reckons the wheel came first of all. Lucía's friend goes further: «The Aztecs didn't even have the wheel, they weren't that smart.»
explain: What really happened: wheeled carts appeared only about 5,500 years ago, long after fire, boats, pots and farming. The Aztecs made little wheeled toy animals, so they knew the idea, but with no horses or oxen to pull carts and a city built on a lake, canoes worked better.
explain: Think of your bike in a muddy forest: sometimes walking is smarter. Hassan's Moroccan gran laughs at his toy truck because in North Africa people once swapped carts for camels, which needed no roads and could cross sand.
explain: Boats are far older than wheels. People reached Australia at least 50,000 years ago across open sea. Tama from New Zealand says his koro, Māori for grandfather, told him their tīpuna, the ancestors, sailed there by the stars: «chur», a Kiwi way to say cheers.
explain: Small things changed daily life too: clay pots for soup, horses for speed, clocks for trains, glasses for old eyes and bicycles that Amara's mum calls «proper rad», meaning really cool, because they gave women freedom to travel on their own.
fact: Wheeled carts appeared about 5,500 years ago, in the Middle East and on the steppes of Eastern Europe, long after farming and pottery.
fact: People in Mexico made wheeled toy animals, but with no big animals to pull carts and steep land and lakes, they used canoes, carriers and roads instead.
fact: About 1,500 years ago, many people in the Middle East and North Africa stopped using carts and moved goods by camel, because camels were cheaper and needed no roads.
fact: The oldest known pottery, found in China, is about 20,000 years old and was made by hunter-gatherers, before farming.
fact: People reached Australia at least 50,000 years ago and must have crossed open sea by boat or raft.
fact: Polynesian navigators sailed double canoes across the Pacific, reading stars, waves, winds and birds, and reached Aotearoa New Zealand about 700 to 800 years ago.
fact: About 2,500 years ago the Persian Royal Road had rest stations where fresh riders and horses carried messages in relays; the Inca later built about 40,000 km of roads with runners.
fact: Scientists using ancient DNA think the horses we ride today were tamed on the steppes north of the Black Sea, around what is now southern Russia and Ukraine, about 4,200 years ago; this is still studied.
fact: The Greek writer Herodotus praised the Persian messengers, saying that neither snow nor rain nor heat nor night stopped them; his words are carved on the main post office in New York.
fact: Until trains in the 1800s, nothing on land went faster than a horse.
fact: Mechanical clocks spread in Europe from about 1300, and China built a great water-driven clock tower in the 1090s.
fact: Each town once set its clocks by the Sun; railways made Britain use one time in the 1840s, and the world agreed time zones from the 1880s.
fact: Eyeglasses were invented in Italy around 1280 to 1290, so people over 40 could keep reading and sewing.
fact: The safety bicycle with two equal wheels and a chain came in the 1880s and let many women travel alone for the first time.
exercise: When did wheeled carts first appear? | About 5,500 years ago | About 300,000 years ago | About 500 years ago | About 50,000 years ago
exercise: Which came before the wheeled cart? | Farming and pottery | Railways and steam engines | Bicycles with pedals | Mechanical clocks in towers
exercise: Why did the Aztecs not use carts? | They had no big animals to pull them, and steep land and lakes | They were not clever enough to work out how a wheel turns | Wheels were banned by their gods, so nobody dared make one | They had never seen anything round, not even in toys
exercise: How did the Aztecs move goods around their lake city? | By canoe and by carriers | By horse and ox cart | By steam train on bridges | By long camel caravans
exercise: Why did people in North Africa swap carts for camels? | Camels were cheaper and needed no roads | Camels were faster than the first cars | Carts were all made of gold and too heavy | Kings banned all wheels just for fun
exercise: How old is the oldest known pottery? | About 20,000 years | About 2,000 years | About 200 years | About 2 million years
exercise: Who made the oldest known pots? | Hunter-gatherers in China | Farmers in Rome | Potters in Victorian London | Pharaohs in Egypt
exercise: How did the first people reach Australia? | By boat or raft across open sea | By walking on dry land all the way | By plane | By riding horses
exercise: What did Polynesian navigators use to find islands? | Stars, waves, winds and birds | A magnetic compass and a map | Maps drawn by European sailors | Only luck and drifting on currents
exercise: When did people reach Aotearoa New Zealand? | About 700 to 800 years ago | About 50,000 years ago, from Australia | About 50 years ago | About 5,000 years ago
exercise: How did the Persian Royal Road carry messages? | Fresh riders and horses in relays | Trained pigeons carrying notes | Letters sent by boat up the rivers | Smoke signals from hill to hill
exercise: Where were the horses we ride today probably first tamed? | On the steppes around southern Russia and Ukraine | In the Amazon rainforest | In Australia | In Antarctica
exercise: What was the fastest way to travel on land before trains? | A horse | A tall bicycle | Walking with a stick | A camel cart
exercise: Why did each town once have its own time? | Each set its clocks by the Sun | Clocks were not invented yet | Kings chose times at random | Every town had a different calendar
exercise: What made Britain use one time for the whole country? | The railways | The first radio broadcasts | The electric telegraph alone | The Roman sundials
exercise: Where and when were eyeglasses invented? | In Italy, around 1280 to 1290 | In China, about 20,000 years ago | In America, in about 1950 | In Egypt, 5,000 years ago
exercise: Why did the safety bicycle matter to many women? | It let them travel alone cheaply | It was the first car anyone could drive | It was only for racing, which women won | It was too heavy for men to ride
test: Owen says the wheel was humanity's first invention. What would a museum guide say? | It came long after fire, boats, pots and farming | Yes, the wheel came even before people used fire | Yes, cavemen rolled stones everywhere before farming | No, the wheel came after cars and trains
test: lucía's mate says aztecs weren't smart cos no wheels. hold up, what's the truth | They knew wheels from toys but carts did not fit their land | They had never seen anything round, not even the Moon lol | They had cars hidden in the jungle the whole time | Nah your mate's right, they couldn't build anything at all
test: In your game, newer tech is always better. Which real choice shows that is not so? | Swapping carts for camels in North Africa | Swapping horses for trains | Swapping candles for bulbs | Swapping scrolls for books
test: Mei says pots came after farming. What did finds in China and Japan show? | Hunters and gatherers made pots first | Pots came only with the first big cities | Pots came to Asia from Rome much later | Pots were never made in Asia before 1500
test: Noah asks if the first Australians just walked there. What is right? | No, there was always open sea; they used boats | Yes, it was all dry land, so they just walked | Yes, they walked over the ice from Antarctica | No, they arrived by plane in the 1900s
test: Some Europeans once said Pacific islands were settled by lucky drifting. What proved them wrong? | Modern voyages in rebuilt canoes, using the old skills | Nothing: scientists have proved that drifting is how it happened | GPS sets found buried inside the old canoes | A Roman map showing every island of the Pacific
test: Ella's uncle says the post office motto is from ancient Persia, mate. What is true? | A Greek writer praised Persian messengers, and New York carved his words | It was written by a Roman emperor for his own letter carriers in Britain | It comes from a 1990s film about a postman in New York | It is from the Inca, who had runners on their roads
test: what if horses had never been tamed. what would change | Travel, farming and war on land would have stayed much slower | Trains would have come 2,000 years earlier to make up for it | Nothing at all, people would just have walked a bit faster | People would have learned to fly on big birds instead
test: Freddie's dad says before railways every town had its own time. Why did trains care? | Timetables need everyone to agree the time | Trains ran on sundials, so they needed sunshine | Trains could only go fast at exactly noon | Trains only ran at night when it was cool
test: Gran says without her glasses she'd have stopped reading years ago. What did glasses change? | People could keep reading and doing fine work after 40 | Everyone could see through walls and far into the night | Nobody needed to read any more after the year 1300 | Children no longer needed school to learn their letters
test: Amara's mum calls women on 1880s bikes proper rad rebels. Why? | Bikes let women travel alone, and some people disapproved | Bikes were only allowed for kings | They rode bikes on the Moon | They invented the car instead
exam: Identify the approximate date of the earliest wheeled vehicles. | Approximately five and a half thousand years ago | Approximately three hundred thousand years ago | Approximately five hundred years ago | Approximately fifty thousand years ago, with the first boats
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the non-use of wheeled carts in Aztec Mexico. | Draught animals were absent and the terrain was unsuitable | The principle of the wheel was wholly unknown in the Americas | Wheels were prohibited by a Spanish decree of the year 1400 | The population was incapable of construction of any kind
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the earliest known pottery? | It was produced by hunter-gatherers in China approximately twenty thousand years ago | It was produced by Roman farmers approximately two thousand years ago for storing grain | It postdates the invention of the wheel by several thousand years | It was introduced together with railways in the nineteenth century
exam: Identify the principal reason for the adoption of a single national time in Britain during the 1840s. | The requirements of railway timetables | The invention of television broadcasting | A decree of the Roman Senate preserved in law | The introduction of eyeglasses into the schools
